    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47112.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 27,
    "turn_text": "But there's a big difference there. And I said this to somebody the other day. I stood up on the floor of the house in 2019 and said, I mean, I don't think it was any sage advice. But I'm like, if you start down this road, one side's never going to unilaterally disarm, Rob. That's not how partisan politics works. uh, there, there's no altruistic version of that, that exists in this town. There never has been, by the way, this is, I mean, this is a new space, but this is not a new phenomenon. I mean, Jeffersonian democracy and Madisonian democracy were two very, very different things. They didn't have Twitter and they didn't have Facebook, so there's a different, they did have poor Richard's Almanac. which was quite possibly the world's first anonymous Twitter account. But, I mean, the reality is, is there is a significant amount of evidence of either, I mean, we have the White House transition team interfering with an FBI investigation of the president's side. We have a DOJ that did nothing to investigate any of these things for four and a half years, and the minute we start asking for things, now they appoint a special counsel. The counterbalance to that is making sure we are in the best position in court possible to get the information we're going to request and have requested and have not been provided. I also will say that nobody cares where we start, period. Nobody cared where the Democrats started. Everybody thought that they did when it was, I mean, those cycles. It matters where it ends. Donald Trump's highest approval rating in the history of his presidency was 49.4%. It was February 1st of 2020. Democrats impeached Donald Trump in December of 2019. It's not enough to follow. And I was a part of all of that. And I think they did it very, very poorly.",
