  {
    "episode_id": 47112,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47112.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 69,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I mean, I'm not I'm not confident we don't end up in some kind of shutdown. I'm working every I'm still in DC. Everybody else is gone. And I'm here and I'm doing and I don't need a pat on the back or any of that. I just want you all to know that I actually take this really seriously. and I think there's a path forward and until, I mean, you know, I've negotiated really tough deals before. Sometimes I was a good defense attorney, but I was never good enough to change the facts. But yeah, I mean, I think we are kind of stumbling our way into this and I want to do I want to give every opportunity I can to keep us out of it. And I think there's a path here. And I think there's a couple policy issues that I think are winners both on a political side and a policy side. And I think we should take advantage of that. I mean, we have a governor of New Jersey who is fighting with Biden administration on immigration. We have a mayor of New York City who is fighting with the Biden administration on immigration. We have a governor of New Mexico who is fighting with the Biden administration on immigration in Massachusetts. Governor just declared a state emergency. I bring all of them up because they are not Republicans. And I think there is a reason that people are frustrated with this. I think you can't be farther away from the southern border than Grand Forks North Dakota. And if you ask anybody in law enforcement, they're what their top three issues are, they will say fentanyl, fentanyl, and fentanyl. And there is a dereliction of duty as to what we are doing to deal with these issues. And if we're going to pick a fight in a CR, it is my personal belief that the right policy fight and the right political fight to pick is on securing the southern border.",
