  {
    "episode_id": 47112,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47112.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 70,
    "turn_text": "No, that makes a lot of sense. And I mean, I think that if there's a unifying theme that runs through all of this, whether it's the government shutdown or the impeachment talks, I think it's unwillingness to sort of get out of our trenches. And what you're talking about is you're pointing out, listen, there are Democrats who are acknowledging that there is a real problem at the southern border, and it's not racist to talk about it. It's not anti-Mexican or anti-immigrant or anything to talk about it and say, this is a problem we have to solve. I mean, I think that's it. How do we get people to come out of their trenches? Because we seem to be living in a political environment where whether we're talking about the North Dakota Republican Party or the national parties, it seems like it's, I mean, we have right now, and I don't know how much you've been paying attention to this, but right now, I mean, there are activists in North Dakota who are touting a legislative scorecard that basically scores bipartisanship to be bad votes. And I feel like that's a manifestation of an attitude where it's like the goal is to just fight the other side, not try to find something we could work with on the other side. How do we get people to climb out of their trenches? Because I just see that there are people who are convinced if you vote with a Democrat, then you're not a conservative and you're a bad person.",
