  {
    "episode_id": 47112,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47112.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 80,
    "turn_text": "Kelly, I'm gonna stay on politics for a second and obviously you're a supporter of Doug Burgum's presidential campaign. He's struggling to get on the debate, the second debate stage. Should he, I mean, you're the former chairman of the GOP and I assume that means you're involved in with the RNC at some point, your professional career. Should Burgum be looking at dropping out of this thing and sort of as a Republican who wants to elect a Republican, should you guys be looking at clearing the field a little bit?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_04",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Doug Burgum is struggling to qualify for the second debate stage.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Kelly is a supporter of Doug Burgum's presidential campaign.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Kelly was formerly the chairman of the GOP.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Kelly has been involved with the RNC at some point in her professional career.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "It is strategically advantageous for Republicans to consolidate support around a single candidate before major election events like debates.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker believes Kelly has influence within the Republican Party due to her past leadership roles.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "A successful Republican presidential nominee requires broad party unity rather than fragmentation.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Burgum’s continued candidacy undermines the overall Republican chances in the general election.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "Dropping out of the race would be seen as a responsible act by a loyal Republican.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Topic Shift"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 47112,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47112.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 81,
    "turn_text": "I think getting on the debate stages is kind of, I mean, particularly when you have front-runners going to both places, but I would never, the longer his voice is in this conversation, I think the better off the country is. That being said, I mean, each and every candidate gets to decide when their path doesn't look like it exists anymore. Those guys spend a lot of time blood, sweat and tears doing that stuff and it's their decision to make. But I think as we continue to go through these processes, the national polling question in general is a little frustrating to me. And I would say this, whether it was Governor Bergam or anybody else. Primaries don't, this goes from being an old political operative, primaries don't run on national polls. I mean, national polling, and if there are people that'll use it, there's some overall predicators that we know exist, but it doesn't particularly matter what you're polling in California. when you're walking into New Hampshire or you're walking into Iowa or you're walking into Nevada or you're walking, maybe Nevada a little bit because they're close, but South Carolina. So I get the metrics. I understand why they're trying to not have to. I mean, I was in 2016 when you had debate A, debate B and this very large field and all of those different issues, but also at the same time, I think metrics based on where you're campaigning at any given time are significantly different than national polling.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_05",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
