    "turn_text": "Well, he's touring the state, going to local district meetings with this legislative scorecard. that he purports to show that that the house, including, by the way, the house leadership, the current, you know, house majority leader, Michael for representative Michael for, you know, the current speaker of the house, Dennis Johnson. that they are, you know, a bunch of liberals. But you really have to understand what he's doing because all the vote does is it just compares how frequently each lawmaker votes with the party from the other side. And it's in terms of evaluating the actual underlying policy. Now I've spoken to a political science professor who says that that sort of analysis is useful if you're trying to compare one legislative chamber to another legislative chamber on how partisan or how polarized those chambers may be. And it turns out North Dakota's legislative chamber is actually not very polarized, right? But in terms of trying to measure is our legislature conservative or is it liberal? It's useless because, or even did they pass good policy or did they pass bad policy because it takes, it does not take into account at all the",
